[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Arrays, Tuples, and Enums (Const vs. Regular) in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript extends JavaScript by adding robust data structures and type safety mechanisms. This tutorial covers the fundamental data structures: Arrays for collections of similar types, Tuples for fixed-length heterogeneous collections, and Enums for defining named sets of constants, including the differences between regular and const enums."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. TypeScript Arrays"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays in TypeScript allow you to store multiple values of the same type. You can define them using two distinct syntaxes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const numbers: number[] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const names: Array<string> = ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"];

// TypeScript prevents assigning wrong types
// numbers.push("six"); // Error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. TypeScript Tuples"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A tuple is a typed array with a pre-defined length and fixed types for each index. Tuples are useful when you want to return multiple values from a function or represent a structured record like a key-value pair."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"let user: [number, string] = [1, "Alice"];

// Valid access
console.log(user[1]); // "Alice"

// Error: Source has 2 element(s) but target allows only 2, and further index mismatches
// user = ["Alice", 1];"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Enums: Regular vs. Const Enums"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums allow developers to define a set of named constants. TypeScript provides two types of enums that compile differently into JavaScript: regular enums and const enums."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regular Enums generate actual JavaScript objects at runtime, allowing bi-directional mapping (name-to-value and value-to-name)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Const Enums are completely removed during compilation. Their usages are inlined with the corresponding constant values, leading to optimized performance and cleaner bundles."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Regular Enum
enum Direction {
  Up,
  Down,
  Left,
  Right
}

let move: Direction = Direction.Up;
console.log(Direction[0]); // Returns "Up" (Reverse mapping)

// Const Enum
const enum StatusCode {
  Success = 200,
  NotFound = 404,
  ServerError = 500
}

let currentStatus: StatusCode = StatusCode.Success;
// Compiled JS will replace 'StatusCode.Success' directly with '200'."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering arrays, tuples, and enums empowers you to write expressive and type-safe TypeScript code. Use standard arrays for homogeneous lists, tuples for strict structural pairing, and const enums for high-performance enumerated constants."}]
